<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#naval-blockade">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Naval blockade</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as blockade</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A naval blockade is the use of warships to stop shipping from reaching or leaving an enemy's ports. Naval supremacy allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> to blockade opponents in its wars and enforce the suppression of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the British blockade caused severe food shortages in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> by 1916–1917, eroding civilian morale.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/naval-blockade.jpg" alt="Naval blockade" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Thomas Luny · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blockade_of_Toulon,_1810-1814.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>A blockade is the use of military force to prevent food, supplies, weapons or communications, and sometimes people, from entering or leaving a country or region. Unlike <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanctions">sanctions</a> or an embargo, which are legal barriers to trade, it is physical. It differs from a siege in being usually directed at a whole country or region rather than a fortress or city, and its aim is not necessarily to conquer the area. Neutral parties must submit to inspection for contraband, which the blockading power may define narrowly or broadly, sometimes including food and medicine. Patrolling hostile ports to keep naval forces from putting to sea is also called a blockade.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Primitive naval blockades have been used for millennia, but early attempts were limited by how long ships could stay at sea without a break. The first successful full naval blockade was made by the British Royal Navy against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. After the British victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay">Quiberon Bay</a> in 1759, Admiral Edward Hawke took command of the fleet off Brest and extended the blockade over the French Atlantic coast from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkirk">Dunkirk</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bordeaux">Bordeaux</a> and to Marseille.</p><p>A distant blockade, in which ships stay well away from the coast, was almost impossible before the 16th century because of the nature of the ships. Its strategic importance became clear in the French Revolutionary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, and the Union blockade of southern ports was a major factor in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>A close blockade places warships within sight of the coast. It is the most effective form and the hardest to keep up, because the ships stay at sea, exposed to storms and sudden attack. A loose blockade withdraws them beyond the horizon to lure the enemy out; Nelson used one at Cádiz in 1805, and the Franco-Spanish fleet under Villeneuve came out and fought at Trafalgar. Until 1827 blockades were always part of a war; the blockade of the Turkish-occupied Greek coast by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, which led to the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-navarino">Navarino</a>, is considered the first pacific blockade.</p><p>Blockades often starved civilians, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biafra">Biafra</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigerian-civil-war">Nigerian Civil War</a>. Since 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-security-council">United Nations Security Council</a> determines their legal status, and modern law treats a blockade used to starve a civilian population as illegal and a war crime. Blockade runners, typically the fastest ships available, carried cargo through such lines.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Blockade” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Bayan I they besieged and took the Byzantine fortress of Sirmium in 582 after a long <b>blockade</b>, then captured Singidunum and raided deep into the Balkans.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the century of naval dominance that followed, Britain suppressed the Atlantic slave trade through the West Africa Squadron, forced open China in the Opium Wars, annexed large territories across Africa and Asia, and maintained imperial communications across the globe without fear of <b>blockade</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-empire">British Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Union <b>blockade</b> then ended commercial shipping, and the government, afraid of hard taxes and collecting only $3.5 million in tariffs in its whole life, financed the war by printing $1.5 billion in notes, which became worthless.</blockquote><a href="/empire/confederate-states-of-america">Confederate States of America · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 451 BCE Cimon led 200 ships to Cyprus and died during the <b>blockade</b> of Citium, though the fleet won a double victory off Salamis, Cyprus, the last major battle against the Persians.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delian-league">Delian League · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The British <b>blockade</b> cut Norway off from Danish grain and caused famine in 1808–1809, and the state went bankrupt in 1813.</blockquote><a href="/empire/denmark-norway">Denmark-Norway · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Naval power was limited, and the Genoese were at times able to <b>blockade</b> or attack the city during commercial disputes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-trebizond">Empire of Trebizond · Military</a></li></ul></section>
